
Java 8 important method to solve the problem

//https://medium.com/@AlexanderObregon/javas-collectors-mapping-method-explained-661ce6ce5bd2
1:Collector.mapping() ->transform elements during a multi-level reduction operation
                            generally used with groupBy/partitioningBy.
public static <T, U, A, R> Collector<T, ?, R> mapping(
    Function<? super T, ? extends U> mapper,
    Collector<? super U, A, R> downstream
)
mapper: A function that transforms elements of type T to type U.
downstream: A collector to accumulate the transformed elements.

it can integrate with groupBy and partitioningBy.

example 1: If you only want a list of employee names (strings) per department rather
than the full Employee objects, you cannot use .map() before groupingBy() because
you would lose access to the department field needed for grouping.
Collectors.mapping() solves this by allowing you to extract the name after or
while grouping.

example 2 :let say using groupBy you can group into let say depart,employee,but you
want to perform operation on let say age so here you can use mapping which can
transform employee type  to age type and let say you want to perform operation like
sort the age and youngest employee so you can do it.
you can also collect in list, set ...

//https://www.linkedin.com/posts/pavanreddy1947_java-javastreams-java8-share-7457406198007607297--eNj/
2:Collectors.collectingAndThen():first collect then transform
  Collectors.collectingAndThen(
   downstreamCollector,   // Step 1: Collect the data
   finishingFunction          // Step 2: Transform the result
  );

lets you apply a final transformation after a collector completes —
think of it as: Collect first → Then reshape the result.
It work similar to mapping as it works with groupBy.

Q: Can collectingAndThen() be replaced with map()?
 👉 No , they operate at different stages:
  map() → transforms individual elements during the stream pipeline
  collectingAndThen() → transforms the entire collected result as a whole

Q: Where is collectingAndThen() most commonly used in real projects?
   👉 Three most common patterns:
    Returning immutable collections from service methods
    Resolving Optional from maxBy / minBy inline
    Formatting values inside groupingBy without extra post-processing
